# PLAN — OODA-buildbox on zebes: 24/7 .oo-native build/audit rig

File: openOODA/docs/ooda-buildbox.oot

Status: v0.5.0 (2026-11-01) — fleet phase implemented. The loop gates every
repository, the workflow contract and the parity gate are written into all 256
fleet repositories, and the fleet backlog names the 152 repositories that hold
no implementation.
As-built: image localhost/ooda-buildbox:latest; quadlet units installed in
/etc/containers/systemd. The unit ooda-buildbox.service is generated and the
hardening drop-in is applied. The timers are NOT enabled yet.
Audit board: openOODA/audit/ooda-buildbox_2026_10_30.oot (186 lines).
Standard: RFC 0010 (fleet rule set and redaction), RFC 0011 (verdict, workflow,
parity, repair). Backlog: openOODA/docs/tools-backlog.oot.

## 1. Goal

Hermetic container on zebes that builds oodac from source, runs the audit gate
on every change, gates every repository of the fleet, and fans diffs out to
spotlight (ollama) for multi-engine review — 24/7, unattended. In-box tooling
is .oo compiled to native binaries; HTTP to spotlight uses the proven
curl-argv pattern. No NEW .sh/.py beyond the rules.oot:146-151 carve-outs.

## 2. Decisions

- D1 — One generated manifest, not a hand written inventory. The manifest is
  derived at loop time from the GitHub API and the local checkout. The rule for
  generating it lives in .oo, so a new repository is picked up without an edit.
- D2 — Fail closed verdict vocabulary. Exactly one of PASS, BUILD_FAIL,
  VERIFY_FAIL, TEST_FAIL, NO_BUILD_TARGET, CI_MISSING, UNVERIFIABLE per
  repository per run. NO_BUILD_TARGET and CI_MISSING are backlog states and
  never successes.
- D3 — The box proposes, the owner merges. Auto-repair ends at an opened pull
  request. The box never merges and never pushes to a default branch. The
  guarantee is enforced by a server side ruleset, not by box discipline.
- D4 — Redaction is a hard precondition. No byte reaches a model until the
  redaction engine has processed it, and a diff that still holds a key shape
  after the redaction is dropped rather than sent.
- D5 — The gate re-checks the whole import closure after any compiler change,
  and the build and emit caches are keyed by the compiler identity. Without
  this the gate certifies a compiler it never exercised.
- D6 — Landing order is: direct push the reviewed audit branches, then the
  workflow branches, then install the review rulesets, then enable auto-repair.
  A direct push and a review ruleset are mutually exclusive.
- D7 — The default branch of every repository comes from the remote. No branch
  name is hardcoded anywhere in the box. The fleet serves main and the core
  repositories serve master, and the box handles both without a code change.

## 3. The blockers that the loop cleared

| ID | Blocker | Fix |
|:---|:---|:---|
| B1 | The branch was hardcoded to master, so every fleet clone failed. | The branch comes from `git ls-remote --symref`. |
| B2 | `OODA_FLEET_ALL=1` was a no-op. | The flag walks the whole organization. `OODA_FLEET=1` uses the published catalog. |
| B3 | The loop never built or tested a tool. | The gate builds, verifies, tests, and writes one verdict per repository. |
| B4 | There was no single flight lock. | An atomic hard link with a stale check. |
| B5 | The structured ERR line was never parsed. | The area and the detail are parsed out of the tool output. |
| B6 | The redaction missed private key blocks and environment dump credentials. | The engine covers both and honours the fake marker. |
| B7 | A push to main produced no release. | The shared auto release workflow is wired into every fleet repository. |
| B8 | 96 repositories had no workflow at all. | Every repository now has the generated contract. |
| B9 | The old workflow could not fail a tool that does not build. | The contract fails on a missing build target and on a missing binary. |
| B10 | RFC 0010 said the fleet is .py/.sh. | The fleet is .oo. RFC 0010 is corrected. |
| B11 | The quadlet used 13 systemd only keys, so the service was never generated. | The keys moved to a service drop-in. The service is generated. |

## 4. The layout

```
buildbox/
  ooda_loop.oo        the entry point
  fanout.oo           the advisory fan out
  selftest.oo         the module self tests
  fleet_apply.oo      writes the contract into every fleet repository
  anchor.oo           the index
  text/               line, word, hash, and JSON helpers
  redact/             the redaction engine and its self test
  fleet/              the verdict vocabulary, the manifest, the gate, the catalog
  loop/               the lock, the compiler identity, the fetch, the manifest,
                      the driver, the GitHub API client
  ci/                 the generated workflow, the upstream package table, the
                      Makefile patch
  parity/             the parity runner, the upstream mapping reader, the case
                      extractor, the self test
```

Every page holds at most 256 lines, at most 8 functions, and at most 40 lines
per function. Every directory holds at most 8 pages. Every file opens with the
four element Academy header, and the header of the parity runner keeps all four
elements inside the first seven lines because the fleet Makefile reads the
header with `head -7`.

## 5. The parity gate

The organization claims strict POSIX and GNU parity for every tool. The parity
gate proves it.

- The upstream program is named in the organization profile for a shipped tool
  and in the fleet backlog for a tool that is not implemented.
- Every repository holds `qa/parity/run.oo` and `qa/parity/cases.oot`. The
  runner is the same file in every repository, so the comparison never drifts.
- The cases come from the test recipe of the repository, so they are the
  arguments the tool already claims to accept.
- A `match` case must produce the same stdout, the same stderr, and the same
  exit code. A `differ` case is a declared deviation and only the exit code
  must match. A case the upstream refuses is skipped.
- The gate fails when the upstream is missing and when the table holds no
  `match` case.

## 6. As-built state

- The loop binary, the fan out binary, the parity runner, the self test, and
  the fleet applier are built and installed in the image build directory.
- The hardening drop-in is applied and `ooda-buildbox.service` is generated.
- The workflow contract and the parity gate are written into all 256 fleet
  repositories on the branch `buildbox/fleet-contract`. Nothing is merged.
- The fleet backlog names the 152 repositories that hold no implementation,
  with the program each one must replace and its tier.
- The timers are not enabled. The enabling is the live change and it comes
  after the rulesets are installed.

## 7. Not done

- The 104 shipped tools are not yet all green. The gate reports the state and
  the repair policy opens pull requests, but the owner merges.
- The 152 stub repositories are not implemented. They are tracked, they fail
  closed, and the published catalog still lists only the shipped tools.
- The rulesets are not installed and the timers are not enabled.
